By \eqref{3.7}, if $z_0$ is a multiple zero of $f$, then $z_0$ must be a zero of $G$. Thus $N_1(r,\frac{1}{f})=T(r,f)+S(r,f)$, where $N_1(r,\frac{1}{f})$ denotes the counting
function corresponding to simple zeros of $f$.
Suppose $z_1 $  is a simple zero of $f$  and not the pole of the  coefficients of \eqref{3.12}, by \eqref{3.12} $z_1$ must be a zero of $(2n-1)Gf''-[(n-1)G'+(a_1+a_3)G]f'$. Let
