\label{sec:betareg}
\subsection{Bayesian parameter estimation}
For estimation we propose taking $b(y|\bx)$ to be the density of the beta distribution $\Be(\psi_1(\bx),\psi_2(\bx))$, for shape functions $\psi_i(\bx;\bgam)$, $i = 1,2$, belonging to a parametric class of functions indexed by a Euclidean vector $\bgam\in \mathbb{R}^k$. Consequently,
%with $\psi_i(\bx) = s(\bx'\bgam_i)$, $i = 1,2$, for a suitable monotonically increasing function $s:(-\infty,\infty) \to (0,\infty)$. Consequently, our full parametric model for $f(y|\bx)$ could be written as (with $t = 0.5$),
\begin{equation}
f(y|\bx) \propto y^{\psi_1(\bx;\bgam)-1}(1-y)^{\psi_2(\bx;\bgam)-1} \times \exp\{-I(y < t)\cdot g(\bx'\balp)\},
\label{eq:par}
