% We consider the time evolution of a neuronal tree represented as a collection of metric graphs $\{\Gamma_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ indexed by time $t$.
We consider the time evolution of a transport network represented as a collection of metric graphs $\{\Gamma_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ indexed by time $t$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:metric_graphs}).
% The (directed) edges $\mathcal{E}_t=\{e_t^i\}$ of $\Gamma_t$ are identified with open intervals directed away from the soma.
A metric graph $\Gamma_t$ is a collection of
the edges $\mathcal{E}_t=\{e_t^i\}$ identified with line segments with Euclidean metric,
and the vertices $\mathcal{V}_t=\{v_t^i\}$ consisting of equivalence classes of the boundary of all the edges $\bigcup_i\partial e_t^i$.
Here, we use the superscript notation for each edge and vertex to indicate the correspondence across the time, i.e., $e^i_t$ corresponds to $e^j_{t'}$ if $i=j$ and vice versa.
In this model, we will only consider a metric graph of which each edge is not a loop; otherwise, we can replace the loop by a cyclic chain of $2$ or more vertices.
For each edge $e^i_t$, we can specify a bond (directed edge) $b_t^i$ and its inverse denoted by $\bar{b}_t^i$ to give directionality to the graph, and we write $\mathcal{B}_t$ the set of all bonds of $\Gamma_t$ and their inverses.
Note that the choice of directionality only matters when there is an advection on the metric graphs.
% We will denote by $x_e=x^i_t$ the local coordinate of each edge $e=e^i_t\in \mathcal{E}_t$.
We will denote by $x^b\in (0,L_e)$ the local coordinate of the bond $b$ of an edge $e\in \mathcal{E}_t$ with edge length $L_e=L_b=L_{\bar{b}}$, and its anti-coordinate by $x^{\bar{b}}$ so that the corresponding location on both bonds are given by the map $\phi$ on $\bigcup_t \mathcal{B}_t$ with $\phi|_b: b\to \bar{b}, x^b \mapsto L_e-x^{\bar{b}}$.
We say that $f:\mathcal{B}_t \to X$ is a ($X$-valued) function on $\Gamma_t$, abbreviated as $f:\Gamma_t \to X$, if $f|_b= f\circ \phi|_b$ for all $b\in \mathcal{B}_t$.
As such, it makes sense to view $f$ on the edges without mentioning the bonds.
% Each $\Gamma_t$ has a unique vertex $v^0$ representing the \textit{soma}.
% Since we are only interested in the cases when $\Gamma_t$ is a metric tree, we shall adopt a convention that $v^i_t$ is the terminal vertex of $e^i_t$ and the soma $v^0$ has incoming degree $0$, i.e., there is no edge with $v^0$ as the terminal vertex.
% We can think of $\Gamma_t$ as an open metric domain whose interior includes the edges, the soma, and the \textit{interior} (non-leaf) vertices.
% The boundary $\partial\Gamma_t$ is the set of the leaves, also called the \textit{tips}.

% We consider intracellular material of concentration $c(\cdot,t):\Gamma_t\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ satisfying
We consider free material of concentration $c(\cdot,t):\Gamma_t\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ satisfying advection-diffusion dynamics:
\begin{equation}
	\partial_t c + \partial_x (a c - D \partial_x c) = s \quad \text{in}\quad \Gamma_t.
	\label{eq:interior}
\end{equation}
This notation is to be understood as follows.
Here, $D(\cdot,t):\Gamma_t\to \mathbb{R}_+$ is the diffusion coefficient, $s(\cdot,t):\Gamma_t\to \mathbb{R}$ is a source (or sink) term, and the advection rate $a(\cdot,t):\mathcal{B}_t\to \mathbb{R}$ satisfies $a = - a \circ \phi$.
The equation \eqref{eq:interior} is to be interpreted in the sense of Euclidean 1D space on each bond, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
	\partial_t c(x^b,t) + \partial_{x^b} (a(x^b,t) c(x^b,t) - D(x^b,t) \partial_{x^b} c(x^b,t)) = s(x^b,t),
\end{equation*}
with imposed \textit{vertex conditions}:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item $c(\cdot,t)$ is continuous in $\Gamma_t$---particularly, at all vertices,
	      % \item the fluxes $J_e := a c - D\partial_{x^e} c$ satisfy Kirchhoff condition at each interior vertex $v$, i.e., \begin{equation}
	\item the fluxes $J_b := a c - D\partial_{x^b} c$ satisfy at each vertex $v\in \mathcal{V}_t$: \begin{equation}\label{eq:flux-VC}
		      % J_{e^i_t}(v^i_t,t) = \sum_{e:\,\mathrm{o}(e)=v_t^i} J_e(v_t^i,t),
		      % \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}^{v,\mathrm{in}}_t} J_e(v,t)
		      % -\sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}^{v,\mathrm{out}}_t} J_e(v,t)
		      \sum_{b\in \mathcal{B}^v} J_b(v,t)
		      = F_v[c],
	      \end{equation} %where $\mathrm{o}(e)$ denotes the origin of an edge $e$.
	      % where $\mathcal{E}^{v,\mathrm{in}}_t$ and $\mathcal{E}^{v,\mathrm{out}}_t$ denote the incoming and outgoing edges associated with $v$, and $F_v$ is a given function.
	      where $\mathcal{B}^{v}$ denotes the outgoing bonds associated with $v$, and $F_v$ is a given functional of the concentration.
	      % which could depend non-locally on the concentration.
	      % which is only non-zero at the growing tips, and identically zero elsewhere.
\end{enumerate}
The simplest case of $F_v$ would be $F_v \equiv 0$ for all $v$,
which corresponds to the so-called Neumann-Kirchoff condition which conserve the total material being transported.
% , given that the edge lengths are fixed.
More generally, when $F_v=\alpha c(v)$ for $\alpha\in \mathbb{R}\cup \{\infty\}$, this is known as $\delta$-type vertex condition is well-studied in the quantum graph theory \cite{berkolaiko_introduction_2013}.
Note that the way we describe \eqref{eq:flux-VC} is so that $F_v$ could depend non-locally on the concentration which allows us to compartmentalize the transport network
and couple the connected compartments back together in a particular way,
as we shall see in the subsequent section \S\ref{sec:growth_at_junction}.

