%\abstract{This paper presents a novel derivation of the score function \(\nabla_y \log p(y)\), where \(p(y)\) represents the probability density function of \(X_T\), the solution at time \(T\) of a general nonlinear stochastic differential equation (SDE). Employing advanced techniques from Malliavin calculus, we extend established results from linear SDEs to tackle the intricacies of fully nonlinear systems. A pivotal contribution of our work is the introduction of a carefully constructed covering vector field, for which we derive an explicit expression for its Skorokhod integral, the Malliavin divergence operator, applied to this process. By leveraging a Bismut-type formula, we establish a rigorous connection between this integral and \(\nabla_y \log p(y)\), enabling us to obtain an explicit and computationally tractable formula for the score function. This formula is expressed solely in terms of the first variation process \(Y_t\) and the second variation process \(Z_t\), with all Malliavin derivatives systematically eliminated, enhancing its practical applicability. Our approach not only deepens the theoretical framework for analysing stochastic systems across a general class of (non)linear SDEs but also holds significant potential for advancing score estimation techniques in generative modelling, thereby facilitating the design of efficient algorithms for sampling from complex probability distributions.}


\abstract{Score-based diffusion generative models have recently emerged as a powerful tool for modelling complex data distributions. These models aim at learning the score function, which defines a map from a known probability distribution to the target data distribution via  deterministic or stochastic differential equations (SDEs). The score function is typically estimated from data using a variety of approximation techniques, such as denoising or sliced score matching,  Hyv\"arien's method, or Schr\"odinger bridges. In this paper, we derive an exact, closed form, expression for the score function for a broad class of nonlinear diffusion generative models. Our approach combines modern stochastic analysis tools such as Malliavin derivatives and their adjoint operators (Skorokhod integrals or Malliavin Divergence) with a new Bismut-type formula. The resulting expression for the score function can be written entirely in terms of the first and second variation processes, with all Malliavin derivatives systematically eliminated, thereby enhancing its practical applicability. The theoretical framework presented in this work offers a principled foundation for advancing score estimation methods in generative modelling, enabling the design of new sampling algorithms for complex probability distributions. Our results can be extended to broader classes of stochastic differential equations, opening new directions for the development of score-based diffusion generative models.}


\keywords{Score function, diffusion models, Bismut formula, Malliavin calculus, stochastic differential equations, generative modelling.}

