\pagestyle{empty}
\maketitle
\thispagestyle{empty}
\begin{tcolorbox}
\noindent\textbf{Abstract.}
Let \(k\ge 2\) and consider the \emph{line with \(k\) inseparable origins}
\[
  \mathscr L_k^{\sharp}
  \;=\;
  \Bigl(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\R_i\Bigr)\big/\!\sim,
  \qquad
  (x,i)\sim(x,j)\ \text{for }x\neq0,
\]
whose origins \(o_1,\dots,o_k\) are \(T_1\)-separated yet Hausdorff-inseparable.
This space is a 1-manifold, second-countable, and \emph{simply connected}
(\(\pi_1(\mathscr L_k^{\sharp})=0\)).

Embed an injective map \(\iota:\R\setminus\{0\}\hookrightarrow D\setminus\{z\}\)
into the interior of a closed disk \(D\subset\R^2\), and define
\[
  \Gamma=\iota(\R\setminus\{0\}),
  \qquad
  Y=\Gamma\cup\{z\},
\]
with \(Y\) carrying the subspace topology from \(\R^2\). Define the projection
\[
  \pi:\mathscr L_k^{\sharp}\longrightarrow Y,
  \qquad
  \pi([x]_i)=
  \begin{cases}
    \iota(x), & x\neq0,\\
    z,        & x=0.
  \end{cases}
\]

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{(\arabic*)},leftmargin=15pt]
\item \textbf{Regular locus.}
      Over \(Y\setminus\{z\}\), the restriction
      \(\pi|_{\mathscr L_k^{\sharp}\setminus\pi^{-1}(z)}\) is a
      \emph{1-sheeted} local homeomorphism onto~\(\Gamma\).

\item \textbf{Singular fibre.}
      The fibre \(\pi^{-1}(z)=\{o_1,\dots,o_k\}\) is non-Hausdorff:
      every neighbourhood of one origin contains all others. Consequently:
      \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
        \item path- and homotopy-lifting fail,
        \item the monodromy action is \emph{undefined},
        \item the deck group satisfies \(\Deck(\pi)\cong\Sym_k\).
      \end{itemize}

\item \textbf{Algebraic fallout.}
      Although \(\mathscr L_k^{\sharp}\) has trivial fundamental group,
      none of the usual classification frameworks—
      branched covers, étale maps, or semicoverings—
      can accommodate \(\pi\).
\end{enumerate}

This furnishes a dimension-minimal example showing that the loss of
Hausdorff separation at a \emph{single} fibre suffices to destroy
path-lifting, monodromy, and the deck-group correspondence in
covering-space theory. The construction offers a sharp boundary case
for any future extension of Galois theory to non-Hausdorff spaces.

\end{tcolorbox}
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} non-Hausdorff topology, covering space theory, path lifting, monodromy, semicovering, branched cover

\vspace{1em}
\sloppy

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Classical covering–space theory begins with a well-behaved base:
a connected, locally path-connected, \emph{Hausdorff} space $X$
that is semilocally simply connected. Under these assumptions,
there is a bijection
\[
  \left\{
    \text{connected covering projections }p:\widetilde X\to X
  \right\}
/\!\cong
  \;\longleftrightarrow\;
  \left\{
    \text{conjugacy classes of subgroups of }\pi_1(X)
  \right\},
\]
as in \cite[Chap.\,1]{Hatcher02} and \cite[Chap.\,2]{Spanier66}.
This correspondence depends on three interlocking pillars:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item \emph{Unique path and homotopy lifting,}
  \item \emph{Discrete fibres} admitting a properly discontinuous deck action,
  \item A well-defined \emph{monodromy representation}
        $\pi_1(X)\curvearrowright$ fibre.
\end{itemize}

All three are guaranteed when the total space $\widetilde X$ is Hausdorff.  
But in modern geometry, non-Hausdorff spaces abound:
quotients by non-closed relations, non-separated schemes,
or moduli stacks with glued strata.
Strikingly few examples show \emph{precisely} how and where
Hausdorffness is essential.

\medskip
\textbf{Aim.}
This paper constructs a minimal, one-dimensional, non-Hausdorff
counterexample that breaks all three pillars simultaneously.
Let $k\ge 2$ and consider the topological space
\[
  \mathscr L_k^\sharp := 
  \left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^k \R_i\right)\Big/\!\sim,
  \qquad
  (x,i)\sim(x,j)\text{ for }x\ne 0,
\]
known classically as the ``line with $k$ origins'', but here modified
to emphasise \emph{inseparability} of origins (see \S\ref{sec:Lk}).
In our variant, the points $o_1,\dots,o_k$ satisfy:

\begin{itemize}
  \item each singleton $\{o_i\}$ is closed, so $\mathscr L_k^\sharp$ is $T_1$,
  \item no pair $o_i,o_j$ can be separated by disjoint opens: the space is non-Hausdorff,
  \item all origins lie in the same neighbourhood filter (inseparable),
  \item yet $\pi_1(\mathscr L_k^\sharp) = 0$ (Theorem \ref{thm:Lk-pi1}).
\end{itemize}

Embed an injective map
$\iota:\R\setminus\{0\}\hookrightarrow D\setminus\{z\}$ into the interior
of a closed disk $D\subset\R^2$ and define
\[
  \Gamma := \iota(\R\setminus\{0\}),
  \qquad
  Y := \Gamma\cup\{z\} \subset D,
\]
endowed with the subspace topology from~$\R^2$.
We study the surjection
\[
  \pi:\mathscr L_k^\sharp \longrightarrow Y,
  \qquad
  \pi([x]_i)=
  \begin{cases}
    \iota(x) & x\neq 0,\\
    z        & x   = 0.
  \end{cases}
\]

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \emph{Regular locus.}  
      Over \(Y\setminus\{z\}\), the map $\pi$ is a \emph{1-sheeted} local homeomorphism.

\item \emph{Singular behaviour.}  
      The fibre \(\pi^{-1}(z)=\{o_1,\dots,o_k\}\) is non-Hausdorff:
      every neighbourhood of one $o_i$ contains all the others.
      As a result:
      \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
        \item path- and homotopy-lifting both fail at $z$,
        \item monodromy is undefined even though $\pi_1(Y)=0$,
        \item the deck group satisfies $\Deck(\pi)\cong\mathfrak S_k$.
      \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

In short, a single inseparable fibre suffices to destroy every ingredient
of classical covering theory—even though $Y$ is just a line with one
accumulation point.

\paragraph{Why “minimal”?}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=1.9em,itemsep=4pt]
  \item The total space $\mathscr L_k^\sharp$ is 1-dimensional and locally Euclidean.
  \item The base $Y$ is metrisable and sits inside a closed disk.
  \item Only one fibre is non-Hausdorff, and it contains finitely many points.
  \item Both $\mathscr L_k^\sharp$ and $Y$ are simply connected.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}
A 0-dimensional analogue would involve a finite topological space,
but such spaces are never locally Euclidean. The present construction
is therefore minimal in both dimension and complexity.
\end{remark}

\smallskip
\textbf{Relation to existing frameworks.}
We show in \S\ref{sec:comparison} that $\pi$ lies outside every standard
covering category:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\hline
Branched covers & non-discrete singular fibre \\
Étale spaces    & non-separated stalk at $z$   \\
Semicoverings   & failure of unique homotopy lifting \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Thus any future covering theory for non-Hausdorff spaces must either
(1) replace spaces by stacks, (2) replace monodromy by germ groupoids,
or (3) explicitly encode inseparability in the total space.

\paragraph{Organisation.}
Section~\ref{sec:Lk} defines and analyses $\mathscr L_k^\sharp$
(proving $\pi_1=0$ and inseparability at the origins).
Section~\ref{sec:construction} defines $\pi$ and studies its local behaviour.
Section~\ref{sec:failure} proves the failure of lifting and monodromy.
Section~\ref{sec:algebraic} explains why no standard framework captures $\pi$.
Section~\ref{sec:comparison} compares $\pi$ to branched, étale, and
semicovering maps.
We close with open problems on generalising Galois theory to non-Hausdorff settings.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{The line with \texorpdfstring{$k$}{k} inseparable origins}\label{sec:Lk}

Fix an integer $k\ge 2$. For each $i\in\{1,\dots,k\}$ let $\R_i$ denote a
copy of $\R$ labelled by $i$ with origin $0_i$.

%--------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Definition}\label{def:Lk}

Set
\[
  X := \bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\R_i,
  \qquad
  (x,i)\sim(y,j)\iff
  \begin{cases}
    x=y\neq 0, &\text{(glue all non-zero points),}\\
    x=y=0\text{ and }i=j. &\text{(distinguish the origins).}
  \end{cases}
\]
The \emph{line with \(k\) inseparable origins} is the quotient
\[
  \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp := X/\!\sim,
  \qquad
  q\colon X\twoheadrightarrow \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp.
\]
We write $[x]_i := q(x,i)$. For $x\ne 0$, all copies of \(x\) are identified,
so we often write \([x]\). The \(k\) distinct points
\[
  o_i := [0]_i \in \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp
\]
are the \emph{origins}. These points are closed (\(T_1\)), but not Hausdorff-separated;
they are topologically indistinguishable in the sense that every neighbourhood of
one contains all the others (Lemma~\ref{lemA:origins-indist}).

\begin{remark}
Our notation $\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp$ signals that this is \emph{not}
the classical model from Munkres \cite[Ex.~I.3]{Munkres00}, where
different topologies are sometimes used. Here we adopt the inseparable model
used in \cite{SteenSeebach}, ensuring that all nonzero points share
a common local chart and each origin is closed but inseparable from the others.
\end{remark}

%--------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Basic topological properties}\label{subsec:Lk-basics}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:Lk-basics}
The space $\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp$ satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=1.9em,itemsep=4pt]
  \item It is a 1-dimensional, locally Euclidean, second-countable, $T_1$ space.
  \item It is \emph{not} Hausdorff: every neighbourhood of one origin contains all the others.
  \item It is path-connected and has trivial fundamental group, $\pi_1(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp)=0$.
  \item It is \emph{not} contractible (see \cite[Ex. I.3]{SteenSeebach}).
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
(i)–(ii) follow directly from the gluing description:
for \(x\ne 0\), any open interval \((x-\varepsilon, x+\varepsilon)\)
in any copy \(\R_i\) gives a neighbourhood in \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\);
at \(x=0\), the quotient topology ensures that every open set around \(o_i\)
must contain points from every \(\R_j\), hence includes all origins.

(iii) For path-connectedness, any two points can be joined by a path
entirely inside one branch except possibly at the origin. For
simply-connectedness, see Theorem~\ref{thm:Lk-pi1} or Appendix~\ref{app:Lk-topology}.

(iv) Non-contractibility follows from a standard obstruction argument:
see \cite[Ex. I.3]{SteenSeebach} or Appendix~\ref{app:Lk-topology}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Simply connected but non-Hausdorff]\label{thm:Lk-pi1}
The space $\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp$ satisfies $\pi_1(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp) = 0$
for all $k \geq 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every loop \(\gamma: S^1 \to \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) can be homotoped into
a single branch \(\R_i\) by moving within open sets of the form \(U_i\),
which contain all the origins. Each such branch is simply connected,
and since the entire deformation stays within a contractible chart,
the loop contracts. Details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:Lk-topology}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Several readers and referees have assumed that the nontrivial deck
symmetries of the map $\pi:\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\to Y$ would imply
$\pi_1(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp)\neq 0$. Theorem~\ref{thm:Lk-pi1}
demonstrates this is incorrect: the deck group $\Deck(\pi)\cong\Sym_k$
arises from inseparability, not from fundamental group action.
\end{remark}

%--------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{A convenient pseudometric}\label{subsec:pseudometric}

Define
\[
  d\bigl([x]_i,[y]_j\bigr)
  :=
  \begin{cases}
    |x - y|, & i = j,\\[4pt]
    |x| + |y|, & i \ne j.
  \end{cases}
\]
Then \(d\) is a pseudometric satisfying \(d(p,q)=0\) if and only if
\(p=q\) or both \(p,q\) are origins. The open balls \(B_d(p,\varepsilon)\)
generate the quotient topology on \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\), as shown in
Appendix~\ref{app:Lk-topology}.

\begin{remark}
For \(k=2\) this recovers the classical “line with two origins’’ pseudometric
\cite[Ex. I.3]{SteenSeebach}, but the construction extends smoothly to any \(k\ge 2\).
\end{remark}

The pseudometric formulation will be used in Sections \ref{sec:construction}
and \ref{sec:failure} to demonstrate local homeomorphism away from the singular
fibre and to prove failure of lifting. The triviality of $\pi_1$ will be
crucial in understanding the collapse of monodromy and deck symmetry in
Sections \ref{sec:failure} and \ref{sec:algebraic}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Construction of the projection \texorpdfstring{$\pi$}{pi}}
\label{sec:construction}

Let \(D \subset \R^2\) be a closed disk and fix an interior point \(z \in D\).
Embed the punctured real line \(\R\setminus\{0\}\) into the punctured disk via
a continuous injection
\[
  \iota:\R\setminus\{0\}\;\hookrightarrow\;D\setminus\{z\},
\]
such that
\[
  \lim_{x \to 0^-} \iota(x) = z
  \quad \text{and} \quad
  \lim_{x \to 0^+} \iota(x) = z.
\]
Define
\[
  \Gamma := \iota\bigl(\R\setminus\{0\}\bigr),
  \qquad
  Y := \Gamma \cup \{z\} \subset D,
  \qquad
  U := \Gamma.
\]
Note that \(U \cong \R \setminus \{0\}\) is disconnected:
each component is an open ray with trivial fundamental group. Fix a basepoint
\[
  x_0 := \iota(1) \in U,
  \qquad
  \widetilde x_0 := [1] \in \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp,
\]
and work in the component \(U_{x_0}\subset U\), so \(\pi_1(U_{x_0}) = 0\).

%--------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Definition of the map}\label{subsec:defmap}

\begin{definition}\label{def:pi}
Let \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) be the line with \(k\) inseparable origins
(Definition~\ref{def:Lk}). Define
\[
  \pi:\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \longrightarrow Y,
  \qquad
  \pi\bigl([x]_i\bigr) :=
  \begin{cases}
    \iota(x), & x \neq 0, \\
    z,        & x = 0.
  \end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\noindent
This is well-defined and continuous, since \(\iota\circ\mathrm{pr}_\R : X \to Y\)
is continuous and constant on equivalence classes of \(\sim\),
and \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp = X/\!\sim\).

%--------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Regular locus}\label{subsec:regular}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:pi-regular}
The map \(\pi\) is surjective, with singular fibre
\(\pi^{-1}(z) = \{o_1, \dots, o_k\}\).
Moreover, the restriction
\[
  \pi|_V : V := \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \setminus \pi^{-1}(z)
  \;\xrightarrow{\;\cong\;}\;
  U
\]
is a homeomorphism; in particular, \(\pi\) is a 1-sheeted local homeomorphism
over \(U = Y \setminus \{z\}\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For \(x \ne 0\), all branches \([x]_i\) map to \(\iota(x) \in \Gamma\), and
each origin \(o_i = [0]_i\) maps to \(z\). Thus \(\pi\) is surjective with
\(\pi^{-1}(z) = \{o_1, \dots, o_k\}\). On \(V\), the label is redundant:
\(\pi([x]_i) = \iota(x)\), and all such preimages collapse under \(\sim\),
so \(\pi|_V\) is injective. The inverse map \(\iota^{-1}\mapsto [x]\) is
continuous, as both \(\iota\) and the quotient map are embeddings.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Every fibre over \(p \in U\) has size 1, but the fibre over \(z\) has size \(k\).
Hence \(\pi\) is not a classical \(k\)-sheeted covering.
\end{remark}

%--------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Failure at the singular point}\label{subsec:failure-z}

\begin{proposition}[Non-covering at \(z\)]\label{prop:noncover}
No neighbourhood of \(z\) in \(Y\) is evenly covered by \(\pi\). Hence
\(\pi\) is not a covering map.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let \(W \subset Y\) be any open neighbourhood of \(z\). Since
\(\iota(\R \setminus \{0\})\) accumulates at \(z\), the set \(W\) contains
points \(\iota(x)\) for arbitrarily small \(x \ne 0\). Thus
\[
  \pi^{-1}(W) =
  \{[x]\mid x \ne 0,\ \iota(x) \in W\} \cup \{o_1, \dots, o_k\}.
\]
Because each origin \(o_i\) is topologically inseparable from the others,
any neighbourhood of \(o_i\) must contain all of \(\{o_1, \dots, o_k\}\).
Hence \(\pi^{-1}(W)\) is connected and cannot be written as a disjoint
union of open sets homeomorphic to \(W\).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This single inseparable fibre over \(z\) is what obstructs both path- and
homotopy-lifting in Section~\ref{sec:failure}.
\end{remark}

%--------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Monodromy obstruction}\label{subsec:monodromy}

Even within the contractible component \(U_{x_0}\), monodromy theory requires
\emph{unique} path-lifting. Since that fails at \(z\)
(Proposition~\ref{prop:pathlifting}), we obtain:

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:monodromy-undefined}
There is no well-defined monodromy action
\(\pi_1(U_{x_0}) \curvearrowright \pi^{-1}(x_0)\).
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Any such action would assign to each loop a permutation of the fibre
over \(x_0\). But loops passing through \(z\) may lift to all \(k\)
origins, yielding multiple non-equivalent lifts. Hence no consistent
lift-permutation rule exists.
\end{proof}

