\section{Failure of Classical Covering--Space Properties}
\label{sec:failure}

Section~\ref{sec:construction} shows that 
\(\pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to Y\) is a \emph{1-sheeted} local homeomorphism over
\(U = \Gamma\) but fails to be a covering at the point
\(z\) because the fibre
\(\pi^{-1}(z) = \{o_1, \dots, o_k\}\) is non-Hausdorff.  
We now record how this single inseparable fibre annihilates
classical properties: unique path lifting, homotopy lifting, monodromy,
and the expected deck-group correspondence.

\smallskip
\noindent
\textbf{Standing notation.}
\[
  U := \Gamma = \iota(\R\setminus\{0\}), \qquad 
  V := \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \setminus \pi^{-1}(z),
  \qquad
  \pi|_V : V \xrightarrow{\;\cong\;} U
  \text{ (by Proposition~\ref{prop:pi-regular})}.
\]

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Failure of unique path lifting}

\begin{proposition}[Non-uniqueness of lifts]\label{prop:pathlifting}
Fix \(x_0 \in U\) with \(x_0 \ne 0\), and let \(\widetilde x_0 = [x_0] \in \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\).
There exists a loop
\[
  \gamma : [0,1] \to Y,
  \qquad
  \gamma(0) = \gamma(1) = x_0,
\]
that passes through \(z\) at \(t = \tfrac{1}{2}\) and admits \(k\) distinct lifts
\(\widetilde\gamma_i : [0,1] \to \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) with \(\widetilde\gamma_i(0) = \widetilde x_0\). 
Hence \(\pi\) fails to have the unique path-lifting property.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let \(x_0 > 0\) and define
\[
  \gamma(t) =
  \begin{cases}
    \iota\bigl(x_0(1-2t)\bigr), & 0 \le t < \tfrac{1}{2},\\[4pt]
    z,                          & t = \tfrac{1}{2},\\[4pt]
    \iota\bigl(x_0(2t-1)\bigr), & \tfrac{1}{2} < t \le 1.
  \end{cases}
\]
Continuity at \(t = \tfrac{1}{2}\) follows since
\(\lim_{t \to \tfrac{1}{2}^\pm} \iota(x_0(1-2t)) = z\). The preimage of \(z\)
under any lift must be some \(o_i\), and after \(t = \tfrac{1}{2}\), the lift
must exit via the corresponding branch. Thus each lift is uniquely determined
by its choice of origin at the midpoint, yielding \(k\) distinct lifts.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monodromy undefined]\label{cor:monodromy}
Although \(\pi_1(U_{x_0}) = 0\), path-lifting is not unique, so no monodromy
action—trivial or otherwise—can be defined on \(\pi^{-1}(x_0)\).
\end{corollary}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Failure of homotopy lifting}

\begin{proposition}[Homotopy-lifting obstruction]\label{prop:homotopy}
There exists a homotopy
\[
  H : [0,1]^2 \to Y
  \quad\text{with}\quad
  H(s,0) = H(s,1) = x_0,
  \quad
  H\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}, t\bigr) = z,
\]
such that no continuous lift
\(\widetilde H : [0,1]^2 \to \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) 
extending the lift of \(H(\cdot,0)\) can exist.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let \(H\) contract the loop \(\gamma\) from Proposition~\ref{prop:pathlifting}
through \(z\). Suppose for contradiction that a continuous lift
\(\widetilde H\) exists with \(\widetilde H(s,0) = \widetilde x_0\). Define
\[
  g(s) := \widetilde H\left(s,\tfrac{1}{2}\right)
  \in \{o_1, \dots, o_k\}.
\]
Since the origins are topologically indistinguishable, any such map \(g\)
must be constant. However, lifting the endpoints of \(\gamma\) yields
\(g(0) = o_i\), \(g(1) = o_j\) for \(i \ne j\), a contradiction. Hence no such
\(\widetilde H\) exists.
\end{proof}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The deck-transformation group}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{deck transformation} is a homeomorphism
\(h : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) satisfying
\(\pi \circ h = \pi\).
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Deck group]\label{prop:deck}
Every permutation \(\sigma \in \mathfrak{S}_k\) of the origins defines a
deck transformation \(h_\sigma\), and
\[
  \Deck(\pi) \;\cong\; \mathfrak{S}_k.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Define \(h_\sigma([x]_i) := [x]_{\sigma(i)}\) for \(x \ne 0\), and
\(h_\sigma(o_i) := o_{\sigma(i)}\). This is continuous and \(\pi \circ h_\sigma = \pi\).
Injectivity follows from \(\sigma \mapsto h_\sigma\) being faithful.
Surjectivity holds since any deck transformation must fix \([x]\) for
\(x \ne 0\), and can only permute the indistinct origins.
\end{proof}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Summary of failures}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:collapse}
For every \(k \ge 2\), the projection \(\pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to Y\) satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\alph*)}, leftmargin=2em, itemsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Local behaviour:} \(\pi|_V : V \xrightarrow{\cong} U\) is a 1-sheeted local homeomorphism.
  \item \textbf{Non-covering at \(z\):} no neighbourhood of \(z\) is evenly covered (Proposition~\ref{prop:noncover}).
  \item \textbf{Path and homotopy lifting fail:} Propositions~\ref{prop:pathlifting} and~\ref{prop:homotopy}.
  \item \textbf{Monodromy absent:} Corollary~\ref{cor:monodromy}.
  \item \textbf{Deck group exotic:} \(\Deck(\pi) \cong \mathfrak{S}_k\), despite \(\pi_1(U) = 0\).
\end{enumerate}
Thus, the classical Galois correspondence between connected covers and
conjugacy classes of subgroups of \(\pi_1(U)\) has no analogue for \(\pi\).
\end{theorem}

%======================================================================
\section{Algebraic Obstructions to Classification}
\label{sec:algebraic}

Section~\ref{sec:failure} exhibited the \emph{analytic} breakdown of
covering theory for the map
\(\pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to Y = \Gamma \cup \{z\}\).
We now show that no standard algebraic framework—subgroups,
groupoids, or semicoverings—captures this map.

\smallskip
\noindent
\textbf{Standing notation.}
We write \(U := \Gamma \cong \R\), fix a point \(x_0 \in U\), and let
\(\widetilde{x}_0 := [x_0] \in \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Fundamental group recap}

From Proposition~\ref{prop:Lk-basics}(iii),
\(\pi_1(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp) = 0\), despite \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) being non-contractible.  
Moreover, the base space also satisfies \(\pi_1(U) = 0\).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{No subgroup realises \texorpdfstring{$\pi$}{pi}}
\label{subsec:no-subgroup}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:no-subgroup}
The classical subgroup correspondence for covering spaces over
\(U\) cannot produce the map
\(\pi : (\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp, \widetilde{x}_0) \to (Y, x_0)\).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since \(\pi\) fails to be a covering at \(z\)
(Proposition~\ref{prop:noncover}), the correspondence is \emph{a priori}
inapplicable. One might still hope to reconstruct \(\pi\) from its restriction
\(\pi|_V \cong U\) together with additional data over the fibre
\(\{o_1, \dots, o_k\}\). However, since \(\pi_1(U) = 0\), the expected monodromy
and deck group would be trivial. In contrast, \(\Deck(\pi) \cong \mathfrak{S}_k\)
(Proposition~\ref{prop:deck}), and no subgroup of a trivial group can account for
this symmetry.
\end{proof}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Failure at the groupoid level}

\begin{definition}
A functor \(F : \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{H}\) between topological groupoids
is a \emph{covering morphism} if it is bijective on objects and each map
\(F : \mathcal{G}(x,x) \to \mathcal{H}(Fx,Fx)\) is a covering map of
topological spaces.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:nogroupoid}
There is no covering morphism
\[
  F : \Pi_1(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp) \to \Pi_1(Y)
\]
with object map given by \(\pi\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix \(x_0 \in U\) and its lift \(\widetilde{x}_0\). Since
\(\pi_1(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp) = 0\), the vertex group
\(\Pi_1(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp)(\widetilde{x}_0, \widetilde{x}_0)\) is trivial.
If \(F\) were a covering morphism, the image inside
\(\Pi_1(Y)(x_0,x_0)\) would have to be discrete. But
\(\Pi_1(Y)(x_0,x_0)\) contains the non-discrete loop space at \(x_0\)
(homeomorphic to \(\R\)), so no such functor can exist.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
We follow \cite{BrownHiggins06} in interpreting \(\Pi_1\) as a
\emph{topological} groupoid, with morphism spaces endowed with the natural
quotient topology from loop spaces.
\end{remark}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Obstruction to semicoverings}\label{subsec:semicovering}

\begin{definition}[{\cite{Brazas11}}]
A map \(p : \widetilde{X} \to X\) is a \emph{semicovering} if it is a local
homeomorphism and both paths and homotopies lift \emph{continuously and
uniquely} from every point of \(\widetilde{X}\).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:not-semicovering}
For every \(k \ge 2\), the projection \(\pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to Y\)
is \emph{not} a semicovering.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:pathlifting} exhibits a loop whose lifts are
non-unique, violating the semicovering axioms.
\end{proof}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Synthesis}

\begin{corollary}[Complete algebraic collapse]\label{cor:algebraic}
None of the standard algebraic frameworks—subgroup classification,
groupoid coverings, or semicoverings—accommodates the map
\(\pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to Y\).
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:no-subgroup},
Proposition~\ref{prop:nogroupoid}, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:not-semicovering}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This obstruction is not due to higher homotopical complexity:
both \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) and \(U\) have trivial \(\pi_1\).
Rather, the failure stems entirely from the single non-Hausdorff fibre,
whose indistinguishable points generate a large deck group
that cannot be anchored in \(\pi_1(U)\).
Section~\ref{sec:comparison} will contrast this with branched covers,
étale maps, and semicoverings.
\end{remark}

%======================================================================
\section{Comparison with Branched, Étale, and Semicovering Maps}
\label{sec:comparison}

Sections~\ref{sec:construction}–\ref{sec:failure} traced every
pathology of \(\pi:\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\!\to\!Y\) to the \emph{single non-Hausdorff
fibre} over \(z\). We now compare \(\pi\) with standard ``cover-like'' frameworks,
identifying precisely which axiom fails in each setting.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Classical branched covers}\label{subsec:branched}

\begin{definition}\label{def:branched-cover}
Let \(X,Y\) be connected \emph{Hausdorff} surfaces. A continuous
surjection \(f:X\!\to\!Y\) is a \emph{branched cover of degree \(k\)}
if every \(y\in Y\) has a neighbourhood \(U\) with
\(f^{-1}(U) = \bigsqcup_{j=1}^{k} V_j\), where each
\(f|_{V_j}: V_j \to U\) is a finite-sheeted covering, branched only at
isolated points.
\end{definition}

Our target \(Y\) fails the \emph{Hausdorff} hypothesis, so
\(\pi\) is disqualified before any local check. Even embedding \(Y\) into a
Hausdorff ambient disk (Appendix~C) does not salvage this:

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:not-branched}
For every \(k \ge 2\), the map \(\pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to Y\) cannot be promoted to a
branched cover of any Hausdorff surface: no neighbourhood of \(z\)
splits into disjoint local sheets.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Every neighbourhood of \(z\) in \(Y\) contains points of \(\Gamma\)
approaching from both sides. Hence \(\pi^{-1}(U)\) is connected for any such
\(U\); a branched cover would require \(f^{-1}(U)\) to be a disjoint
union of local sheets.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
A genuine branched cover carries local monodromy; for \(\pi\), monodromy is
\emph{undefined} (Corollary~\ref{cor:monodromy}), so the obstruction is intrinsic.
\end{remark}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Étale spaces of presheaves}\label{subsec:etale}

Let \(\Sh(Y)\) denote the topos of sheaves of sets on \(Y\).

\begin{definition}
A map \(p : E \to Y\) is an \emph{étalé space} if it is a local
homeomorphism. It is \emph{separated} iff \(E\) is Hausdorff—equivalently,
distinct germs over a point are topologically distinguishable.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:not-separated-etale}
The restriction \(\pi|_V : V \xrightarrow{\cong} U\) is a local homeomorphism, so
\(\pi\) is étale on a dense open. However, \(\pi\) is \emph{not} the
étalé space of a separated sheaf, because the stalk over \(z\) is
inseparable.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
Consider the presheaf assigning to each open \(W \subset Y\) the set of locally constant maps
on \(W \setminus \{z\}\) labelling the branch index in \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\).
Two distinct sections labelled \(i \ne j\) coincide on \(W \setminus \{z\}\),
so the separatedness condition fails.
\end{proof}

Thus \(\pi\) is \emph{quasi-étale} (étale off a closed set) but not
Grothendieck-étale.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Semicoverings (Brazas)}\label{subsec:semico}

\begin{definition}[{\cite[Def.~2.1]{Brazas11}}]
A map \(p : \widetilde{X} \to X\) is a \emph{semicovering} if it is a local
homeomorphism and every path and homotopy in \(X\) lifts
\emph{continuously and uniquely} from each point of \(\widetilde{X}\).
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:not-semico}
\(\pi\) is not a semicovering.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:pathlifting}, \(\pi\) admits a loop with
\(k\) distinct lifts, contradicting unique path lifting.
\end{proof}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Stacks and future frameworks}\label{subsec:stacks}

Current definitions of topological and differentiable stacks require
\emph{separated} atlases. Since \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) is non-Hausdorff at the
origins, \(\pi\) cannot be realised as a stacky cover under existing definitions.
A future theory of \emph{non-Hausdorff stacks}—capable of distinguishing
points that are topologically inseparable—would be needed to
accommodate projections like~\(\pi\).

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Summary}\label{subsec:summary}

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l c@{}}
\hline
Framework & Axiom that fails for \(\pi\) & Holds? \\
\hline
Classical covering & Evenly covered nbhd at \(z\) & \(\times\) \\
Branched cover     & Hausdorff target \emph{and} disjoint sheets & \(\times\) \\
Étale space        & Separated stalk at \(z\) & \(\times\) \\
Semicovering       & Unique path/homotopy lifting & \(\times\) \\
Stacky cover       & Hausdorff atlas (separated) & \(\times\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Single-axiom breakdowns for
\(\pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \to Y = \Gamma \cup \{z\}\).}
\end{table}

\begin{remark}
In every framework, the underlying obstruction is the same: the
\(k\) origins form a \emph{non-separable} fibre. Hausdorffness of the
total space—often assumed silently—is indispensable for any
Galois-type classification of coverings.
\end{remark}
%======================================================================
\section{Conclusion and Outlook}\label{sec:conclusion}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Main findings}

For each \(k \ge 2\), we constructed a continuous surjection
\[
  \pi : \mathscr{L}_k^\sharp \longrightarrow Y := \Gamma \cup \{z\},
\]
formed by collapsing the embedded image curve \(\Gamma \subset D \setminus \{z\}\)
to the point \(z\). Away from \(z\), the map is a \emph{1-sheeted} local homeomorphism,
yet possesses a single non-Hausdorff fibre
\(\pi^{-1}(z) = \{o_1, \dots, o_k\}\).

Sections~\ref{sec:construction}–\ref{sec:comparison} establish the following:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \(\mathscr{L}_k^\sharp\) is locally Euclidean, second-countable, \(T_1\), and has
        trivial fundamental group, but is \emph{not} contractible and
        fails Hausdorff separation precisely at the origins.
  \item Unique path- and homotopy-lifting both fail for paths meeting
        \(z\); consequently, classical monodromy is undefined.
  \item The deck-transformation group is large:
        \(\Deck(\pi) \cong \mathfrak{S}_k\), despite
        \(\pi_1(U) = 0\).
  \item \(\pi\) fits into none of the classical frameworks—branched covers,
        étale maps, or semicoverings—each of which requires
        either Hausdorff fibres or unique lifting.
\end{enumerate}

Thus a \emph{single non-separated fibre} suffices to annihilate every
pillar of the classical Galois correspondence, even when the base is as
tame as a punctured real line.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Why Hausdorffness is indispensable}

Classical covering theory rests on three interlocking properties:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Unique path and homotopy lifting}, enabling a monodromy action;
  \item \textbf{Discrete fibres}, allowing a faithful deck transformation group;
  \item \textbf{Evenly covered neighbourhoods} at \emph{every} point.
\end{itemize}

The map \(\pi\) violates the first two at a single fibre, which in turn
precludes the third. In that precise sense, the example is
\emph{minimal}: one notch down in the separation axioms collapses the
entire classification superstructure.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Towards a non-Hausdorff covering theory}\label{sec:future-theory}

Extending covering-space theory beyond the Hausdorff world may require:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item \emph{Encoding inseparable fibres.}  
      A richer object—perhaps a topological stack or groupoid with
      ``smeared'' isotropy—must retain information about indistinguishable
      yet distinct lift choices.
\item \emph{A softened Galois correspondence.}  
      Instead of a group acting \emph{faithfully} on a discrete fibre,
      one may need an action of a \emph{groupoid of germs}, or a
      higher-categorical analogue that counts admissible lifts without
      enforcing uniqueness.
\end{enumerate}

A promising prototype may be a Deligne–Mumford–type stack with
non-separated inertia, blending stack theory with coarse
(non-separated) geometry.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Open problems}\label{subsec:open}

\vspace{-0.2\baselineskip}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item \textbf{Finite non-Hausdorff fibres.}  
      Classify all connected, locally trivial surjections over a
      punctured surface whose singular fibres are finite but
      non-Hausdorff.

\item \textbf{Higher-dimensional analogues.}  
      Does an \(n\)-manifold analogue of the line-with-\(k\)-origins
      exist for \(n > 1\), locally Euclidean except along a codimension-2
      non-Hausdorff stratum?

\item \textbf{Stack-theoretic Galois theory.}  
      Develop a correspondence between subgroupoids of a
      ``topologised'' \(\pi_1\)-stack of the base and suitable
      \emph{stacky covers} whose total spaces may be non-Hausdorff.

\item \textbf{Shape-theoretic invariants.}  
      Identify shape or pro-homotopy invariants that detect the failure
      of unique lifting in non-Hausdorff covers.
\end{enumerate}

