The primary contribution of this paper is the integration of Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization into forward variable selection and the proposal of a new model size selection rule, leading to the development of the GSFR method. Unlike existing ultra-high dimensional variable selection techniques that rely on marginal contribution metrics, our approach selects variables based on their unique contribution to the response. This refinement not only reduces multicollinearity but also improves model accuracy by prioritizing the most informative features. Moreover, GSFR achieves a convergence rate at least comparable to that of OGA, satisfies the sure screening property, and ensures variable selection consistency under mild conditions. The computational efficiency and theoretical guarantees make our method a robust and effective approach for ultra-high dimensional variable selection. 

Beyond linear models, GSFR can be applied in nonlinear time series modeling, including the identification of multivariate threshold autoregressive (AR) models and the detection of structural breaks, among many others. For example, \citet{chan2015lasso} constructs a new design matrix through a sorting operation, transforming multiple-threshold detection into a regression-based variable selection problem. GSFR can be performed efficiently to threshold estimation after a simple modification that incorporates group structures, as proposed by \citet{chan2017threshold}. Similarly, structural break detection can be reformulated as a variable selection problem by introducing truncated feature variables into the regression model, as discussed in \citet{StructuralBreaksinTimeSeries}. If the coefficients corresponding to these variables can be consistently estimated and identified as nonzero, the associated break points can be accurately located. Under this formulation, GSFR can be directly employed to achieve consistent estimation and precise break point detection.


The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews the Orthogonal Greedy Algorithm (OGA) and Forward Regression (FR), presents counterexamples to motivate our proposed GSFR approach, and provides a detailed introduction to the algorithm. Section 3 discusses the theoretical properties of GSFR. Section 4 evaluates the numerical performance of the proposed approach through simulations and a real data example. Section 5 concludes. All proofs and derivations for the asymptotic results are provided in an online Appendix.




