\subsection{Real Data Analysis}
\hspace{2em} In this section, we illustrate the proposed methodology using a real data set {conerning} the riboflavin production in Bacillus subtilis. The data contain \(n = 71 \) samples, where the response variable is the logarithm of the riboflavin production rate, and the covariates are the logarithm of expression levels of \(p = 4081 \) genes. More descriptions about the dataset can be found in \citet{buhlmann2014high}. The production manager is interested in identifying which genes have the most significant impact on riboflavin production while accounting for the effects of other genes. Thus, the regression model (\ref{model}) serves as a valuable tool for optimizing production strategies, facilitating more effective resource allocation, and enhancing production efficiency. Before analysis, all covariates are standardized to have zero means and unit standard deviations.

 We randomly select \(n = 27\) (approximately one-third of the data) as the test set (\citet{wang2009forward}), using the remaining data for training. We then evaluate the average performance of the three original methods (GSFR, FR, and OGA) based on 1000 random experiments. The relevant parameters, such as \( K_n\) and the adjustment terms, are set to be the same as those used in the simulation. Since we cannot observe the true model in the real dataset, we assess the out-of-sample performance using the average selected model size and MSPE as the evaluation metrics, and use running time to evaluate computational efficiency.

