\section{Conclusion}


This paper {introduced} a GSFR method for ultra-high dimensional variable selection. Unlike existing methods that rely on marginal contribution measures, GSFR iteratively selects variables by evaluating their unique contributions using a dynamically updated candidate set, thereby mitigating multicollinearity and improving selection accuracy. The method achieves a convergence rate at least comparable to OGA, satisfies the sure screening property, and ensures selection consistency under mild conditions. With its computational efficiency and theoretical guarantees, GSFR emerges as a reliable and effective approach for ultra-high dimensional variable selection. 

Current results of GSFR are established under the assumption of light-tailed distributions. Heavy-tailed distributions can lead to inaccurate Pearson correlation estimates and reduce selection accuracy. As a potential extension, since rank correlations such as Spearman’s rho are more robust to heavy tails and outliers, replacing Pearson correlations with rank correlations in the selection criterion could enhance the robustness of GSFR. Furthermore, this study focuses on variable selection for standard linear regression models. GSFR can be extended to generalized linear models (GLMs) using the method in \citet{jiang2024decorrelated} to convert GLMs to Model (\ref{model}), thereby covering logistic, Poisson, and other models. These directions provide a basis for future work to extend and strengthen the scope of GSFR.


%This paper introduced a GSFR method for ultra-high dimensional variable selection. Unlike existing methods that rely on marginal contribution measures, GSFR iteratively selects variables by assessing their unique contributions using a dynamically updated candidate set, mitigating multicollinearity and improving selection accuracy. The method attains a convergence rate at least comparable to OGA, satisfies the sure screening property, and ensures selection consistency under mild conditions. Its computational efficiency and theoretical guarantees establish GSFR as a reliable and effective approach for ultra-high dimensional variable selection. Extensive simulations demonstrate that GSFR consistently outperforms existing methods in variable selection accuracy. An application to riboflavin production data, where gene expression levels serve as predictors, further confirms its superior predictive performance with an appropriately chosen model size. Overall, GSFR provides another efficient solution for variable selection in ultra-high dimensional settings.



























