\subsection{Final Sample and Trends}

In the following section, we will be comparing our theoretical models with the Lupus and USco disks, representing respectively young and old populations. We give a brief summary of these disks here.

Our revised radii for the USco disks, almost by construction, place them on the same SLR as for other younger clusters \citep[Fig. \ref{fig:sizel revised} here, also see][for Taurus and Ophiuchus]{Tazzari2021}. If correct, disks across all ages follow an SLR that has a normalization and slope broadly expected of optically thick emission (Fig. \ref{fig:sizel revised}). This also explains the  same low spectral indexes across all regions.
An exception is found in the largest disks which tend to have higher spectral indexes, suggesting that their optical depths may start to thin down. 

Comparing to the younger populations, USco disks are smaller, hardly any larger than 40 au. This may lead one to infer that disks dissipate outside in and that large disks become small over time. On the other hand, USco also have a lower  disk frequency \citep{Ribas2014}. So it is also possible large disks simply disappear faster, leaving only initially compact systems. 


