% The method described so far does not scale to typically used long training processes since it involves 

\textit{Influence Functions~\citep{koh2017understanding}:} DataInf represents an efficient algorithm for computing influence functions, distinguished by its closed-form expression that reduces computational and memory complexity compared to existing methods. The algorithm approximates the inverse Hessian calculation $(G_l(\theta^*) + \lambda_l I_{d_l})^{-1}$ through the key transformation: $\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\left(\nabla_{\theta_l}\ell_i\nabla_{\theta_l}\ell_i^T + \lambda_lI_{d_l}\right)^{-1} \approx \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\left(I_{d_l} - \frac{\nabla_{\theta_l}\ell_i\nabla_{\theta_l}\ell_i^T}{\lambda_l + \nabla_{\theta_l}\ell_i^T\nabla_{\theta_l}\ell_i}\right)$, where the Sherman-Morrison formula enables a closed-form solution. The influence function is computed as $\mathcal{I}_{\text{DataInf}}(x_k, y_k) = \sum_{l=1}^L \frac{1}{\lambda_l}\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{L_{l,i}}{\lambda_l + L_{l,ii}}L_{l,ik} - L_{l,k}\right)$.%, achieving $\mathcal{O}(\sum_{l=1}^L nd_l)$ computational complexity with $\mathcal{O}(\max_{l\in[L]} d_l)$ memory requirements. % This efficiency makes DataInf particularly advantageous over traditional methods like LiSSA, eliminating the need for iterative operations and Hessian matrix storage while maintaining theoretically bounded approximation error that scales as $\mathcal{O}(d_l^2)$ when first-order gradients and $\lambda_l$ are bounded.


