For the reconstruction method adopted in this work: %, we proceed as follows: 
($i$) we reconstruct the $H(z)$ function using CC data via the \texttt{ReFANN} code (see Section \ref{rec_method}) and use it in Eq. \eqref{e2}, and ($ii$) we use the FRB data to constrain the model's parameters, namely, $\{H_0, e^{\mu}, \sigma_{\rm host}\}$. The MCMC procedure performed to estimate the parameters is structured as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item [(I)] We adopt the fiducial value $\Omega_{\rm b}=0.0494$ provided by the Planck 2018 \cite{aghanim2020planck} and assume a flat prior over $H_0$ with a wide range [60, 80], which encompasses the measurements of Planck 2018 and SH0ES \cite{aghanim2020planck,riess2022comprehensive}. Next, the best-fit values for $H_0$, $e^{\mu}$ and $\sigma_{\rm host}$ are calculated. This stage represents the process of reconstruction.

    \item [(II)] To include additional probes, we combine the result for $H_0$ obtained in (I) with $H_0$ derived from SNe and BAO data (referred to as DESI in the contour plots). In the BAO case, we impose a Gaussian prior on the baryon density, $\Omega_{\rm b}h^2 = 0.02218 \pm 0.00055$ \cite{schoneberg20242024}. For the absolute magnitude, we adopt a Gaussian prior with $M_b=-19.2\pm 0.04$ following \cite{camarena2021use}. In this context, the models for SNe and BAO assume the $\Lambda$CDM model, and the parameters to be fitted are $H_0$ and $\Omega_{\rm m}$. Finally, we compare the $H_0$ values obtained from individual samples and their combinations. 
\end{itemize}

For the cosmography approach, the following steps are performed: 

\begin{itemize}
    \item [(I)] Flat priors are assumed for $\Omega_{b}h^2$ and $H_0$ with intervals [0.017784, 0.031616] and [60, 80], respectively. The best-fit values of $H_0$, $q_0$, $j_0$, $e^{\mu}$, and $\sigma_{\rm host}$ for FRB, and $H_0$, $q_0$, $j_0$ for BAO, SNe and CC are calculated. These parameters are estimated separately for the FRB, SNe, BAO, and CC datasets.

    \item [(II)] Additionally, a joint analysis combining FRB, SNe, BAO, and CC data is performed to constrain the shared parameters $H_0$, $q_0$, and $j_0$.
\end{itemize} 


\section{Constraints with reconstruction of $H(z)$}\label{sec6}

This section presents the results obtained by the \texttt{ReFANN} reconstruction method, which provides $H(z)$ based on the real dataset. The breakdown consists of the posterior distributions and parameter correlations derived from the MCMC analysis with FRB data. The three free parameters explored are the Hubble constant $H_0$, the median of the log-normal distribution $e^\mu$, and the host galaxy uncertainty $\sigma_{\text {host }}$. The two-dimensional contour plots display $68 \%$ and $95 \%$ confidence levels, with weak correlations between most parameters, suggesting that the constraints on $H_0$ are not strongly degenerate with uncertainties, which does not affect the estimate of the Hubble constant. The marginalized one-dimensional posteriors shown in Fig. \ref{fig:1} indicate well-constrained estimates for the Hubble constant: $H_0=69.9 \pm 5.8 \mathrm{~km} \mathrm{~s}^{-1} \mathrm{Mpc}^{-1}$ with an accuracy of $\sim 8\%$. Despite the mean value being closer to the Planck value, this result is also compatible with the one derived by the SH0ES team at a $1\sigma$ confidence level. This finding reduces the error found by the authors in Ref. \cite{Fortunato:2024hfm} ($H_0=68.2\pm7.1 \mathrm{~km} \mathrm{~s}^{-1} \mathrm{Mpc}^{-1}$), where an approach using a neural network was employed. There, they used a dataset comprising 21 localized data points. The increase in the number of FRBs for 116 leads to a higher precision for $H_0$. 

