Compared to these works, our analysis provides a notably tighter constraint on both $e^{\mu}$ and $\sigma_{\rm host}$, with uncertainties reduced by factors of $\sim 3$--$4$, and with a central value of $e^{\mu}$ that is significantly higher. Our recovered $\sigma_{\rm host} = 0.804^{+0.065}_{-0.071}~\mathrm{pc}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ also reflects a broader but well-constrained host DM fluctuation compared to the fixed or strongly prior-driven models adopted in previous studies. Our findings suggest that explicitly assuming a cosmological model in Eq. \eqref{e2} increases uncertainty and decreases the mean of $e^{\mu}$. Moreover, the level of agreement in $H_0$ across all methods, despite the differences in modeling assumptions, reinforces the robustness of FRB-based cosmological constraints. However, our tighter constraints and less assumption-dependent approach highlight the added value and complementarity of neural-network-based methods in FRB cosmology. 

