To quantify the tension between two independent measurements of a cosmological parameter, such as the Hubble constant \( H_0 \), we use the standard tension metric defined as the absolute difference between the mean values divided by the quadrature sum of their standard deviations. Assuming approximately Gaussian posteriors, the tension in units of standard deviations (\( \sigma \)) is given by:
\begin{equation}
T = \frac{|\mu_1 - \mu_2|}{\sqrt{\sigma_1^2 + \sigma_2^2}},
\end{equation}
where \( \mu_1 \) and \( \mu_2 \) are the means, and \( \sigma_1 \) and \( \sigma_2 \) are the standard deviations of the two measurements, respectively. Values of \( T \gtrsim 3\sigma \) are typically interpreted as indicating significant tension. Fig. \ref{fig:3} presents a matrix of statistical tension in units of $\sigma$ between the inferred values of the Hubble constant from the datasets used in the previous analysis and their combinations. The SNe dataset exhibits significant tension, more than $3\sigma$, with DESI, FRB+DESI, and the combined datasets. The tension between SNe and BAO was also ascertained by the DESI collaboration \cite{DESI:2025zgx}. FRBs show low tension with all other probes, which indicates their consistency as potential, independent, and complementary cosmological probes.

