\section{Constraints with cosmography}\label{sec7}

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{figures/cosmo1.pdf}
    \caption{\justifying Two-dimensional marginalized contours for the localized FRB sample with $68.3\%$ and $95.4\%$ confidence levels for cosmography. These findings were obtained considering a flat prior for $\Omega_bh^2$ and a fixed value for $f_{\rm IGM}$.}
    \label{fig:4}
\end{figure}

In this section, we discuss the findings regarding the cosmographic approach. In the following, we present the results for FRBs alone and then for SNe, BAO, CC, and their combinations. Fig. \ref{fig:4} unveils the marginalized posterior distributions for the parameters adjusted from the FRB sample. The contours illustrate the one- and two-sigma confidence intervals for the parameters $q_0$, $j_0$, $H_0$, $e^{\mu}$, and $\sigma_{\rm host}$. The marginalized one-dimensional posteriors for each parameter are shown along the diagonal. The first point we should highlight is the Hubble constant obtained through this method, $H_0=65.83_{-4.87}^{+3.77} \mathrm{~km} \, \mathrm{s^{-1}} \, \mathrm{Mpc^{-1}}$, representing a precision of $\sim 6\%$. This yields a lower value compared to the one previously found by the reconstruction method. It is relevant to point out that the mean is lower than the one from the Planck Collaboration, although the uncertainty lies within the Planck value at a $1\sigma$ confidence level. Notably, this outcome is not compatible with the one found by the SH0ES team. Moreover, the best-fit for the deceleration parameter is $q_0=-0.45_{-0.31}^{+0.26}$ (with an accuracy of $\sim 60\%$), corroborating a phase of accelerated expansion of the Universe, as well as with the $\Lambda\rm CDM$ model. In this case, the jerk parameter is such that $j_0=1.17_{-0.58}^{+0.70}$ with $\sim 49\%$ of precision, suggesting that FRBs alone cannot indicate a non-standard cosmology description, favoring the $\Lambda\rm CDM$ model, where $j_0=1.00$. 

Moreover, the best fits for the log-normal distribution parameters are $e^{\mu}=88.65_{-14.47}^{+14.05}$ and $\sigma_{\mathrm{host}}=0.89_{-0.13}^{+0.11}~\mathrm{pc}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$, with depicting precisions $\sim 16\%$, and $\sim 13\%$, respectively. The correlation coefficient between these parameters is $\rho=-0.57$, indicating a negative correlation. This correlation was not observed in the reconstruction approach. Notably, the parameters $e^{\mu}$ and $\sigma_{\mathrm{host}}$ do not present any significant correlation with $H_0$, as shown in the contours of Fig. \ref{fig:4}. Likewise, the parameters $q_0$ and $j_0$ do not exhibit significant correlations with $H_0$ nor with each other. A comparison between the reconstruction and cosmographic approaches reveals substantial differences in the estimated parameters. For the parameter $e^{\mu}$, the reconstruction yields a value that is approximately 135\% higher than the one derived from cosmography, with slightly larger associated uncertainties (up to 18\% higher). In contrast, the estimates for the host dispersion $\sigma_{\mathrm{host}}$ are in closer agreement, with the reconstruction value being about 10\% lower. However, the uncertainties in $\sigma_{\mathrm{host}}$ are smaller in the reconstruction method, reduced by over 40\% in both upper and lower bounds. These discrepancies reflect the sensitivity of each method to the modeling assumptions and represent different levels of constraining power.

To assess the predictive power of FRBs alongside other probes in the realm of cosmography, we conducted a joint analysis using data from FRB, SNe, CC, and BAO from DESI and their combinations. Table \ref{tab:cosmo_results} displays the best fits for each probe, as well as those for the joint analysis. The individual constraints from SNe, CC, and DESI unveil distinct levels of precision for the cosmographic parameters. The SNe dataset provides the most precise determination of the Hubble constant, $H_0 = 73.09^{+1.42}_{-1.36} \,\mathrm{~km} \, \mathrm{s^{-1}} \, \mathrm{Mpc^{-1}}$, corresponding to a relative uncertainty of 1.89\%. DESI also yields a highly precise estimate, $H_0 = 65.20^{+1.29}_{-1.28}~ \mathrm{~km} \, \mathrm{s^{-1}} \, \mathrm{Mpc^{-1}}$, with a relative uncertainty of 1.93\%, while the CC data give a less precise result at the 5.24\% level ($H_0 = 68.81_{-3.51}^{+3.60}~ \mathrm{~km} \, \mathrm{s^{-1}} \, \mathrm{Mpc^{-1}}$). For the deceleration parameter, SNe again perform best, achieving $q_0=-0.42_{-0.07}^{+0.08}$ with a relative uncertainty of about 16.2\%, followed by DESI with $q_0=-0.29\pm 0.07$ ($\sim 24\%$) and CC with $q_0=-0.56\pm 0.23$ (38.5\%). Regarding the jerk parameter, DESI stands out with an exceptionally tight constraint, $j_0 = 0.58^{+0.03}_{-0.04}$ (relative uncertainty $\sim6.8\%$), in contrast to the significantly higher uncertainties from SNe ($j_0 = 0.64^{+0.38}_{-0.46}$ with 61.7\%) and CC ($j_0 = 1.00^{+0.67}_{-0.66}$ with 56.8\%). Note that only the DESI measurement provides a statistically significant indication of deviation from the $\Lambda$CDM prediction of $j_0 = 1$, suggesting possible departures from standard cosmology at a $1\sigma$ confidence level.

