\subsection{Impulsive gLV - disturbances} Consider the Lotka-Volterra models with impulsive disturbances introduced in Section~\ref{sec:LV}. For these models, Section~\ref{sec:LV:ri} showed that for any ergodic measure $\mu$, the invasion growth rate $r_i(\mu)$ equals $\left(\sum_j a_{ij} x_j^*(\mu)+b_i\right)\tau+\log(1-d_i)$ where the average densities $x_i^*(\mu)$ correspond generically to the unique solution of a system of linear equations \eqref{eq:LV:x*}. Importantly, as the solutions of these linear systems are unique, assumption \textbf{A6} holds generically. As arbitrarily small perturbations of the model parameters $a_{ij},r_i,d_i$ ensure that the average per-capita growth rates $r_i(\mu)$ are non-zero for species not supported by $\mu$ (i.e. $i\notin I(\mu))$, assumption \textbf{A7} also holds generically.

The solutions of \eqref{eq:LV:x*} and the invasion growth rates $r_i(\mu)$ correspond to the non-negative equilibria and associated invasion growth rates of the following autonomous Lotka-Volterra equations:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LV:autonomous}
\frac{dx_i}{dt}=x_i\left(\sum_j \tau a_{ij}x_j + \tau b_i+ \ln(1-d_i) \right)
\end{equation}
However, it is an open question whether, in general, an equilibrium $x^*$ of \eqref{eq:LV:autonomous} supporting species $I\subset[k]$ implies the existence of an ergodic measure $\mu$ for the flow-kick system satisfying $I(\mu)$. Consequently, the invasion graph $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick}}$ for the flow-kick system is a subgraph of the invasion graph $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ for the autonomous system \eqref{eq:LV:autonomous}. Specifically $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick}}$ could miss some of the vertices of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ and the corresponding edges into and out of these missing vertices. As $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick}}$ is a subgraph of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$, it follows that if $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ is acyclic and satisfies the invasion condition, then $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick}}$ is acyclic and satisfies the invasion condition, and consequently the flow-kick system is permanent. The autonomous invasion graph for $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ can be solved using the algorithm introduced in \citet{hofbauer-schreiber-22}

Which vertices of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ are also vertices of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick}}$? One class is vertices corresponding to subsystems satisfying the permanence conditions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main2}. Specifically, let $I\subset [k]$ correspond to a vertex of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ and define $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto},I}$ to be the subgraph of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ with all vertices $J\subset [k]$ of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto}}$ such that $J\subseteq I$ and all the corresponding edges. If $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny auto},I}$ is acyclic and satisfies the invasion criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:main2}, then the same holds for the subgraph $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick},I}$ of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick}}$. Consequently, the subsystem of the flow-kick system corresponding to the species in $I$ is permanent. It follows that there is a fixed point $z^*\in \S_I$ on the kick-to-kick map and, consequently, an ergodic measure $\mu=\delta_{z^*}$ with $I(\mu)=I$. Hence, $I$ is a vertex of $\mathcal{G}_{\mbox{\tiny flow-kick}}$. 

