\section{Computational considerations}

\subsection{Computing $r_i(\mu)$}

\subsection{Lyapunov exponents and $r_i(\mu)$}\label{sec:lyapunov}

The goal of this section is also to describe how the invasion growth rate for a structured population corresponds to a dominant Lyapunov exponent.

Population matrix models - focusing only on one of the species
\begin{equation}\label{eq:structured:flow}
\begin{aligned}
   \frac{du}{dt} = &a(w) u  \qquad \mbox{where }w=(u,v)\\
   \frac{dv}{dt}=& b(w)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Let $w.t$ denote the solution to \eqref{eq:structured:flow} with initial condition $w$. Let $\tau(w)>0$ be the time until the next kick given the previous kick lead to state $w$, and let the kick map be
\[
w=(u,v)\mapsto (A(w)u,B(w))=:C(w)
\]
Then the kick-to-kick map for this system is 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:structured:kick2kick}
w(n+1)=K(w(n)) \mbox{ where }K(w)=C(w.\tau(w))
\end{equation}

Now, consider an initial condition $w=(0,v)$ where the focal species is not present. Let $\Phi(t,w)$ be the solution valued in the matrix $k\times k $ to \[ \frac{d\Phi}{dt}=a(w.t)\Phi \], that is, the fundamental matrix for this linearized system. If introduced at infinitesimally small densities $\widetilde u\approx$, then the dynamics of the focal species from kick-to-kick is approximated by the non-autonomous linear system
\begin{equation}\label{eq:structured:linearized}
\widetilde u(n+1)=A(w(n).\tau(w(n))\Phi(\tau(w(n)),w(n))\widetilde u(n)
\end{equation}
where $w(n)=(0,v(n))$ is the solution \eqref{eq:structured:kick2kick}. Provided that $A(w.\tau(w))\Phi(\tau(w),w)$ is nonnegative and has a fixed sign pattern of a primitive matrix for all $w$, \citet{roth-schreiber-2014} implies that there exists a real number $\chi(w)$ such that 
\[
\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\ln \| \widetilde u(n)\|=\chi(w)
\] 
for all $\widetilde u(0)\in \R^k_+\setminus \{0\}$. Moreover, if $\mu(dw)$ is an ergodic measure for \eqref{eq:structured:kick2kick} supported on $u=0$, then there exists $\chi(\mu)$ such that 
\[
\chi(w)=\chi(\mu) \mbox{ for }\mu \mbox{ almost every }w=(0,v).
\]
$\chi(\mu)$ is known as the dominant Lyapunov exponent of linearized dynamics \eqref{eq:structured:linearized}. In the special case where $\mu$ corresponds to a periodic orbit $w(0),w(1),\dots, w(N)=w(0)$ to \eqref{eq:structured:kick2kick} with $w(0)=(0,v(0))$, $\xi(\mu)$ equals $\frac{1}{N}\ln \lambda$ where $\lambda$ is the dominant eigenvalue of the primitive matrix:
\[
A(w(N-1).\tau(w(N-1))\Phi(\tau(w(N-1)),w(N-1))\dots A(w(0).\tau(w(0))\Phi(\tau(w(0)),w(0))
\]

To relate this Lyapunov exponent to the average per-capita growth rate for a model of the form $\eqref{eq:kick-to-kick}$, one needs to make a change of variables $(x,\rho)$ where $x$ corresponds to the density of the focal population and $\rho$ is the distribution of individuals across the $k$ stages i.e. 
\[
x=\sigma(u):=\sum_i u_i \mbox{ and } \rho=u/x 
\]
If we set $y=(\rho,v)$, then the flow in these coordinates is 
\[
\begin{aligned}
   \frac{dx}{dt} = &\sigma(a(x\rho,v)\rho) x \\
   \frac{d\rho}{dt}=& a(x\rho,v)\rho - \sigma(a(x\rho,v)\rho) \rho\\
   \frac{dv}{dt}=&b(x\rho,v)
\end{aligned}
\]
and the kick is 
\[
(x,\rho,v)\mapsto \left(\sigma(A(\rho x,v)\rho)x,\frac{A(\rho x,v)\rho}{\sigma(A(\rho x,v)\rho)},B(\rho x,v)\right)
\]
This defines a kick-to-kick dynamics of the form \eqref{eq:kick-to-kick}. Now let $r(\nu)$ be the average per-capita growth rate of the focal species with respect to an ergodic measure $\nu(dz)$ not supporting the focal species i.e.  $\nu(\{0\}\times \R^k)=1$. This ergodic measure pulls back to an ergodic measure $\mu$ in the $(u,v)$ coordinate system such that $\mu(\{(0,v)\}=1)$. Then $r(\nu)=\chi(\mu)$

