For size ratios $r>2$, an interesting observation from the DEM simulations is that the evolution of small particles appears nearly identical across different cases, whereas the behavior of large particles varies noticeably (figure~\ref{fig:size_effect_r_1.25_1.5_2_2.5_3}b). Our model captures the differences in the large particle dynamics well, but it does not reproduce the nearly identical evolution of the scaled center of mass position for the small particles (figure~\ref{fig:size_effect_r_1.25_1.5_2_2.5_3}c). However, the model does reasonably capture the $y_{com}$ profiles for mixtures with compositions of $f^T_L = 0.25$ and $f^T_L = 0.75$ at $r = 2.5$ and $3.0$ (see figure~\ref{fig:flt_0.25_0.75_r_2.5_3} of the supplementary material).
%The continuum model predictions (solid lines) show reasonable agreement with the DEM data (symbols) even for these higher size ratios, although the differences between the model and DEM data become more significant for higher size ratios. These results suggest that particle force-based segregation model successfully predicts the evolution of the flow and segregation behavior for binary size mixtures over a wide range of compositions and size ratios for different inclination angles and initial configurations.
