\section{$\mu - I$ and $\phi - I$ rheology}
\label{sec:mu_I_phi_I}
The shear stress ($\tau_{yx}$) and pressure ($P$) in dense granular flows are related by means of the effective friction coefficient dependent on the inertial number $\mu(I_{mix}) = |\tau_{yx}|/ P$. 
The JFP model \cite{jop2006constitutivenature} empirically relates the variation of $\mu$ with $I$ as 
\begin{equation}
\mu(I_{mix}) = \mu_{s}+\frac{\mu_{m}-\mu_{s}}{1+I_{0}/I_{mix}}, 
\label{eq_5:mu_I_JFP}
\end{equation}
where, $\mu_s$, $\mu_m$, and $I_0$ are the rheological model parameters. The expression of the inertial number for the granular mixture having identical density ($\rho_{mix} = \rho_p$) and different size ($d_{mix} = \sum_i d_i f_i$) particles is given by $ I_{mix}=|\dot\gamma| d_{mix} /\sqrt {P/\rho_p}$, where $\dot \gamma =dv_x/dy$ is the local shear rate. As a first approximation, the local solids fraction $\phi$ can be obtained using the dilatancy law for monodisperse grains using the generalized inertial number \cite{tripathi2011rheology} as 
\begin{equation}
    \phi_{th}(y,t)=\phi_{max}- \beta I_{mix}. 
    \label{eq:packing_fraction}
\end{equation}
The parameters $\phi_{max}$ and $\beta$ of the dilatancy law, obtained from the DEM simulation data, are the same as those used for the monodisperse case.
The values of these parameters are taken from \cite{tripathi2013density,sahu_kumawat_agrawal_tripathi_2023} and are reported in table~\ref{tab:rheopar}.
