In sum, our analysis demonstrates that policy-influential scientists in climate modeling exert a disproportionate impact on policy not simply because they study salient topics, but because their professional stature—reflected in IPCC authorship and other forms of visibility—amplifies the uptake of their work. Even after controlling for topical similarity, PI-Sci papers receive markedly more policy citations, and text mining reveals a division of labor in which PI-Sci contribute high-level modeling frameworks while non-PI-Sci supply crucial observational and problem-identification research. Together, these findings highlight the importance of both scientific content and scientific prominence in shaping how climate knowledge informs policy.


